[{:db/id 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5d565601-7981-50a3-bc57-df1008cb679e" "question-8cd9aa05-4a21-5975-bf09-f09975b43169" "question-73ab53ad-67cd-53a7-b27f-c766bd840711"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119668 :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. ![](images/62/62e90ac708f5a7d2942d328a1dcbcfc435e6cf900b71f0564307e020d822748b.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/94/94db4ea75dacbd6aaf0ab0e32a6f871bc6e50c8029da7c6800a49762feb2f560.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/e4/e4b8dd6cb97a21581eb6d2cc2ddcd6028917aeff8d76cd55185acb995ff3074c.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n![](images/e1/e1b3730913029ed000c4ef86338bcd8be0fff045cda12131f894a1818996521b.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n![](images/a7/a7d5c905d9f78444b00c51a1b641d132365f5fc8619ad00fc0fd5fc27942709c.svg)\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n![](images/f1/f1d0656f8aabffd2d3e2bee903f3763aa610a4731991669a625e473262d7ea32.svg)\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n![](images/00/00c52c08ef614e87fe29584d994cec16298fa1f3bf02a1b4c6b7463bede7a6d1.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119603 :question/problem "Given ![](images/e3/e34daf05ba492435f014fedd8336dd4bb4c65ae9c1688947d7873b8fba3b3e22.svg) find its LU decomposition." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-07a8a0f6-ff41-525d-a016-8184036d8c7d" "question-a069a9c2-f0e9-5f4e-8079-7e889bf03a9b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119604 :question/problem "Given \n![](images/d5/d5f838110aeb104064744379d4879fb237a2ad9ad7b4728f4216d9f1bd39cd8a.svg), find its LU decomposition." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5d565601-7981-50a3-bc57-df1008cb679e" :question/id #uuid "5d565601-7981-50a3-bc57-df1008cb679e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106752" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99b3cb96-646b-5a28-8673-dce2e304d7b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99b3cb96-646b-5a28-8673-dce2e304d7b9" :answer-field/id #uuid "99b3cb96-646b-5a28-8673-dce2e304d7b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-59f68716-0281-55f6-adea-b682838ac2b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-95e30625-bcfc-5d55-98a0-5886f8f651fb" "answer-73eed6d2-2a7a-5586-96c3-b4b57d716ee8" "answer-85f1cd67-ccaa-5df2-b149-878d29a40002" "answer-59f68716-0281-55f6-adea-b682838ac2b7" "answer-a96911ae-7ad9-5ac1-b6d0-ca8ccdeddc8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95e30625-bcfc-5d55-98a0-5886f8f651fb" :answer/id #uuid "95e30625-bcfc-5d55-98a0-5886f8f651fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73eed6d2-2a7a-5586-96c3-b4b57d716ee8" :answer/id #uuid "73eed6d2-2a7a-5586-96c3-b4b57d716ee8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85f1cd67-ccaa-5df2-b149-878d29a40002" :answer/id #uuid "85f1cd67-ccaa-5df2-b149-878d29a40002" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59f68716-0281-55f6-adea-b682838ac2b7" :answer/id #uuid "59f68716-0281-55f6-adea-b682838ac2b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a96911ae-7ad9-5ac1-b6d0-ca8ccdeddc8e" :answer/id #uuid "a96911ae-7ad9-5ac1-b6d0-ca8ccdeddc8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186345030 :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120188 :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117289 :answer-field/correct 17592186117292 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-8cd9aa05-4a21-5975-bf09-f09975b43169" :question/id #uuid "8cd9aa05-4a21-5975-bf09-f09975b43169" :question/math-academy-id "q-32611" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains numbers different from $1.$\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-407f9dd1-cf10-5a35-8a2f-095674048c4c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-407f9dd1-cf10-5a35-8a2f-095674048c4c" :answer-field/id #uuid "407f9dd1-cf10-5a35-8a2f-095674048c4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d54070f0-d20a-5ea6-aa9b-5964baf98a82" :answer-field/choices ["answer-55cc3f2d-2c9d-518f-83cf-c0d0c9b25d1f" "answer-374fcecf-456e-59e1-810b-f5c42633df35" "answer-64d9eef5-56f0-5943-8389-0af8adc56d25" "answer-d54070f0-d20a-5ea6-aa9b-5964baf98a82" "answer-289d8b11-5b33-5df3-83e4-bfbab449cfa4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55cc3f2d-2c9d-518f-83cf-c0d0c9b25d1f" :answer/id #uuid "55cc3f2d-2c9d-518f-83cf-c0d0c9b25d1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-374fcecf-456e-59e1-810b-f5c42633df35" :answer/id #uuid "374fcecf-456e-59e1-810b-f5c42633df35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64d9eef5-56f0-5943-8389-0af8adc56d25" :answer/id #uuid "64d9eef5-56f0-5943-8389-0af8adc56d25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d54070f0-d20a-5ea6-aa9b-5964baf98a82" :answer/id #uuid "d54070f0-d20a-5ea6-aa9b-5964baf98a82" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-289d8b11-5b33-5df3-83e4-bfbab449cfa4" :answer/id #uuid "289d8b11-5b33-5df3-83e4-bfbab449cfa4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-73ab53ad-67cd-53a7-b27f-c766bd840711" :question/id #uuid "73ab53ad-67cd-53a7-b27f-c766bd840711" :question/math-academy-id "q-75115" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a6784eb-df62-5231-a320-70204c921944"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a6784eb-df62-5231-a320-70204c921944" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a6784eb-df62-5231-a320-70204c921944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bffe5c32-5854-51db-b8f4-05b3f68585e1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a875e35-4a03-5b66-9640-19be64465b55" "answer-5cd73843-fd8e-5e43-8c1d-d6377ae8623a" "answer-3f014fff-e379-5421-b2b4-49d5a54f23a1" "answer-bffe5c32-5854-51db-b8f4-05b3f68585e1" "answer-48377761-7467-51b7-97c9-f6247717ea78"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a875e35-4a03-5b66-9640-19be64465b55" :answer/id #uuid "6a875e35-4a03-5b66-9640-19be64465b55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cd73843-fd8e-5e43-8c1d-d6377ae8623a" :answer/id #uuid "5cd73843-fd8e-5e43-8c1d-d6377ae8623a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f014fff-e379-5421-b2b4-49d5a54f23a1" :answer/id #uuid "3f014fff-e379-5421-b2b4-49d5a54f23a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bffe5c32-5854-51db-b8f4-05b3f68585e1" :answer/id #uuid "bffe5c32-5854-51db-b8f4-05b3f68585e1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48377761-7467-51b7-97c9-f6247717ea78" :answer/id #uuid "48377761-7467-51b7-97c9-f6247717ea78" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120182 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=-1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117253 :answer-field/correct 17592186117254 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120186 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117277 :answer-field/correct 17592186117281 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-07a8a0f6-ff41-525d-a016-8184036d8c7d" :question/id #uuid "07a8a0f6-ff41-525d-a016-8184036d8c7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-32619" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=4,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-29b866c9-e8f1-5844-9f9f-7ff1d2ebf13b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-29b866c9-e8f1-5844-9f9f-7ff1d2ebf13b" :answer-field/id #uuid "29b866c9-e8f1-5844-9f9f-7ff1d2ebf13b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c55e82fd-3868-524e-a176-4ae0eb02d2d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef91d311-f5cd-5c1f-8b87-7a75202de12a" "answer-94171c94-68a7-5ef2-aaba-7694ee1ad0d9" "answer-91f3cfba-511f-5a32-b5ed-37b88de9cce4" "answer-c55e82fd-3868-524e-a176-4ae0eb02d2d1" "answer-1fb42e2c-aa24-5f95-88b6-df41338ccd5f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef91d311-f5cd-5c1f-8b87-7a75202de12a" :answer/id #uuid "ef91d311-f5cd-5c1f-8b87-7a75202de12a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94171c94-68a7-5ef2-aaba-7694ee1ad0d9" :answer/id #uuid "94171c94-68a7-5ef2-aaba-7694ee1ad0d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91f3cfba-511f-5a32-b5ed-37b88de9cce4" :answer/id #uuid "91f3cfba-511f-5a32-b5ed-37b88de9cce4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c55e82fd-3868-524e-a176-4ae0eb02d2d1" :answer/id #uuid "c55e82fd-3868-524e-a176-4ae0eb02d2d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fb42e2c-aa24-5f95-88b6-df41338ccd5f" :answer/id #uuid "1fb42e2c-aa24-5f95-88b6-df41338ccd5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a069a9c2-f0e9-5f4e-8079-7e889bf03a9b" :question/id #uuid "a069a9c2-f0e9-5f4e-8079-7e889bf03a9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106772" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=5,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a0f52f38-93c1-5335-86f8-ba552718ba99"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a0f52f38-93c1-5335-86f8-ba552718ba99" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0f52f38-93c1-5335-86f8-ba552718ba99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-af51841d-a19a-5772-9383-ea7b8a9aa1a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-238ca86a-9d68-5724-a14e-22dd68af5c77" "answer-d4df948f-38dc-5e81-b2b2-2938f23c6e53" "answer-4c31da62-609b-5d8a-8854-0a6fa3314fc7" "answer-6c9c5aa7-a098-5b4d-b132-40b681368c9f" "answer-af51841d-a19a-5772-9383-ea7b8a9aa1a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-238ca86a-9d68-5724-a14e-22dd68af5c77" :answer/id #uuid "238ca86a-9d68-5724-a14e-22dd68af5c77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4df948f-38dc-5e81-b2b2-2938f23c6e53" :answer/id #uuid "d4df948f-38dc-5e81-b2b2-2938f23c6e53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c31da62-609b-5d8a-8854-0a6fa3314fc7" :answer/id #uuid "4c31da62-609b-5d8a-8854-0a6fa3314fc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c9c5aa7-a098-5b4d-b132-40b681368c9f" :answer/id #uuid "6c9c5aa7-a098-5b4d-b132-40b681368c9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af51841d-a19a-5772-9383-ea7b8a9aa1a3" :answer/id #uuid "af51841d-a19a-5772-9383-ea7b8a9aa1a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121028 :tutorial/content "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121067 :tutorial/content "Let ![](images/ff/ff7b3f4bca1676cbe3fb78ae1c2a47d8fd0bc71dd98dee4c7b6fea4cc94ec7bd.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/3f/3f74fdde04340f6a4dbd86962f5a31f8af22b5fb8053c66fa3e8be41629b12a1.svg)\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120001 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
